Building the LTSP6 lab from scratch - a reproducible recipe
=============================================================

This is the order the lab was actually built in, with the commands that
worked and the traps that cost time. Follow it top to bottom and you get:
thin clients that network-boot into a graphical login, authenticate
against a Samba AD domain, and land in an XFCE session on one of several
application servers behind a load-balanced, highly available RDP entry
point - plus a separate staging environment for validating changes
before they reach everyone.

Read "Traps, and how each one announces itself" at the end before you
start. Every entry there is something that presented as a different
problem than it was.


What you end up with
------------------------

    thin client
        |  PXE: proxy DHCP -> TFTP -> iPXE -> kernel+initrd
        |  root filesystem over NBD, copied into RAM (toram)
        v
    ldm greeter (GTK3)  -- verifies the password with the KDC first
        |
        |  RDP to the keepalived VIP
        v
    lb1 / lb2 (HAProxy + keepalived, one VIP each for prod and staging)
        |
        +--> ltsp-app1 ---+
        +--> ltsp-app2 ---+--> /home over NFS from home1
        +--> ltsp-app3 (staging pool only)
                 |
                 v
        addc1 (Samba AD DC): Kerberos, LDAP, DNS

Roles are separated on purpose. ltsp-root builds and serves the client
image and answers PXE; it runs no user sessions. The application servers
run sessions and never build or serve images. One application server per
hypervisor node, so the load balancer has genuinely independent backends.


Address plan
----------------

Everything that another machine names by address is static. This is not
tidiness: DHCP lease changes broke the corosync quorum and then the
keepalived pair in this very lab, both times presenting as something
else entirely.

    172.17.250.10   addc1        Samba AD DC, DNS for the domain
    172.17.250.20   ltsp-root    image server, NBD, TFTP, proxy DHCP
    172.17.250.31   ltsp-app1    application server (node 1)
    172.17.250.32   ltsp-app2    application server (node 2)
    172.17.250.33   ltsp-app3    application server, staging pool
    172.17.250.40   home1        file server, /srv/home over NFS
    172.17.250.51   lb1          HAProxy + keepalived, MASTER
    172.17.250.52   lb2          HAProxy + keepalived, BACKUP
    172.17.249.222  VIP_PROD     RDP entry point for everyone
    172.17.249.223  VIP_STAGING  RDP entry point for the pilot group

    realm LTSPLAB.LOCAL / domain ltsplab.local, gateway 172.17.240.1,
    /20 network, no IPv6 anywhere internally.


Prerequisites
-----------------

  - Two Proxmox VE nodes (this lab ran them nested inside Hyper-V; a
    physical pair is easier and skips the checksum-offload trap below).
  - A Debian netinst ISO on each node, in /var/lib/vz/template/iso.
  - xorriso on whichever node builds the install media.
  - The scripts from server/tools in this repository.
  - Roughly 12 GB of RAM across the two nodes for the full set, and
    about 8 GB of disk per guest. Watch the node that holds the image
    server: image builds are what fills it up.


Step 1 - Build the auto-install media
-----------------------------------------

Clicking through debian-installer once per guest does not scale, and
rebuilding a guest later should not depend on anyone remembering which
answers they gave. server/tools/build-autoinstall-iso turns a netinst
image plus a preseed into a medium that installs unattended and powers
itself off when done.

The preseeds in server/tools/preseed encode the role in their package
list - that is where role separation actually lives:

    addc1.cfg      samba, krb5, dns
    ltsp-root.cfg  debootstrap, squashfs-tools, nbd-server, tftpd-hpa,
                   dnsmasq, ethtool, rsync - and no desktop
    ltsp-app.cfg   xrdp, xorgxrdp, xfce4, xscreensaver, sssd, adcli,
                   krb5-user, nfs-common, ethtool - and no build tools

The hostname is per instance. Build one medium per guest, changing only
that line:

    cd /root
    for host in addc1 ltsp-root ltsp-app1 ltsp-app2 ltsp-app3; do
        case $host in
            addc1)     src=preseed/addc1.cfg ;;
            ltsp-root) src=preseed/ltsp-root.cfg ;;
            *)         src=preseed/ltsp-app.cfg ;;
        esac
        sed "s|^d-i netcfg/get_hostname string .*|d-i netcfg/get_hostname string $host|" \
            "$src" > "/root/$host.cfg"
        ./build-autoinstall-iso \
            /var/lib/vz/template/iso/debian-13.6.0-amd64-netinst.iso \
            "/root/$host.cfg" "$host"
    done

Two things this script had to learn the hard way. It replaces
isolinux/isolinux.cfg outright rather than adding an include, because a
"default" inside an included fragment does not win - the first attempt
booted the speech-synthesis installer instead. And it takes the isohybrid
MBR from the source ISO, so the result boots on BIOS as well as UEFI.


Step 2 - Create the guests
------------------------------

Nothing surprising here; the point is which guest goes where. Put one
application server on each node, and lb1 and lb2 on different nodes, so
neither failover story depends on a single host.

    # node 1
    qm create 202 --name addc1     --memory 1536 --cores 2 ...
    qm create 210 --name ltsp-root --memory 2048 --cores 2 ...
    qm create 211 --name ltsp-app1 --memory 2048 --cores 2 ...
    qm create 200 --name lb1       --memory 1536 --cores 1 ...
    # node 2
    qm create 212 --name ltsp-app2 --memory 2048 --cores 2 ...
    qm create 213 --name ltsp-app3 --memory 1536 --cores 2 ...
    qm create 201 --name lb2       --memory 1536 --cores 1 ...
    pct create 220 --hostname home1 ...

Full form for one of them, for reference:

    qm create 213 --name ltsp-app3 --memory 1536 --cores 2 \
        --net0 virtio,bridge=vmbr0 --scsihw virtio-scsi-single \
        --scsi0 local:8,format=qcow2 \
        --ide2 local:iso/ltsp-app3-auto.iso,media=cdrom \
        --boot order='ide2;scsi0' --ostype l26

Start it, wait for it to power itself off, then detach the medium and
boot from disk:

    qm start 213
    # ... wait until: qm status 213 -> stopped
    qm set 213 --ide2 none,media=cdrom
    qm set 213 --boot order='scsi0'
    qm start 213

Do not over-commit memory. Ten gigabytes of guests on four gigabytes of
host does not fail loudly; it just swaps until everything is too slow to
diagnose.

The file server can be an unprivileged container only if you do not need
the kernel NFS server in it. This lab used a privileged container for
exactly that reason.


Step 3 - The domain controller
----------------------------------

Everything else authenticates against this, so it comes first.

    provision-samba-ad-dc 172.17.250.10/20 172.17.240.1 \
        LTSPLAB.LOCAL LTSPLAB '<admin-password>'

The script pins the static address, fixes /etc/hosts, provisions the
domain with --use-rfc2307, sets a DNS forwarder, points the host at
itself for resolution and verifies the result.

Two details it exists to get right. It refuses to continue if the host
holds more than one address, because a DC that answers DNS from an
address the clients did not ask sees every reply discarded. And it sets
the DNS forwarder rather than checking whether the line exists - the
first version checked for the presence of "dns forwarder" and happily
left it at "none".

Verify before moving on:

    samba-tool domain level show
    host -t SRV _ldap._tcp.ltsplab.local
    host -t SRV _kerberos._udp.ltsplab.local
    kinit administrator && klist


Step 4 - The image server
-----------------------------

    setup-ltsp-root 172.17.250.20 ens18 172.17.240.1 172.17.250.10

This sets up the NBD export, dnsmasq for proxy DHCP plus PXE plus TFTP,
the checksum-offload workaround, and rewrites nbdroot= in the iPXE
script to point at this host.

Proxy DHCP matters: the network already has a DHCP server, and running a
second authoritative one alongside it is how you get two servers
answering the same request. In proxy mode dnsmasq answers only the PXE
part and leaves addressing alone. One catch - with "port=0" dnsmasq
advertises no resolver at all, which stalls the Debian installer at
"Name server addresses"; hand out option:dns-server explicitly.

The iPXE script that ends up in /srv/tftp:

    #!ipxe
    kernel /vmlinuz initrd=initrd.img root=/dev/nbd0 \
        nbdroot=172.17.250.20:10809/ltsp-amd64 ro ip=dhcp \
        init=/sbin/init-ltsp console=tty0 console=ttyS0,115200n8 \
        random.trust_cpu=on toram
    initrd /initrd.img
    boot || goto failed

"toram" copies the image into tmpfs and disconnects NBD, so a client
survives losing the image server. Confirm it took effect on a booted
client: /run/toram/root.img mounted on /run/initramfs/rofs, and
NBD_DISCONNECT in the log.

And the NBD export:

    [ltsp-amd64]
        exportname = /srv/ltsp/images/amd64.img
        readonly = true


Step 5 - Build the client image
-----------------------------------

The image is a plain debootstrap tree, squashed. No LTSP build tooling is
installed on the server; this keeps the recipe legible and means nothing
hidden happens between "install a package" and "clients get it".

    B=/srv/ltsp/build/unsq
    debootstrap --arch=amd64 trixie "$B" http://deb.debian.org/debian
    mount --bind /dev "$B/dev"; mount -t proc proc "$B/proc"
    mount -t sysfs sys "$B/sys"

    chroot "$B" apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        linux-image-amd64 initramfs-tools systemd-sysv \
        xserver-xorg-core xserver-xorg-video-all xinit x11-xserver-utils \
        kbd pciutils console-setup keyboard-configuration \
        freerdp3-x11 nbd-client openssh-client
    # the ldm packages built from the ldm-mabutz repository
    chroot "$B" dpkg -i /tmp/ldm_*.deb

Then the two configuration files that decide whether any of this works.
/etc/lts.conf in the image:

    [default]
        LTSP_CONFIG=True
        LDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND="xfreerdp"
        RDP_SERVER="172.17.249.222"          # VIP_PROD
        RDP_OPTIONS="/cert:ignore /dynamic-resolution /sound /clipboard"
        XKBLAYOUT=de
        CONSOLE_KEYMAP=de
        RDP_PREAUTH=True
        RDP_PREAUTH_REALM=LTSPLAB.LOCAL

XKBLAYOUT is not cosmetic. Without it the client runs a US layout while
the user types on a German keyboard, and any password containing a
character that moves between the two arrives wrong - "-" typed on QWERTZ
reaches the server as "/". The domain rejects it as a wrong password with
no hint that the layout is to blame. This cost an hour.

RDP_PREAUTH exists because xrdp has no server-side NLA. Without it a
wrong password does not end the connection: xrdp accepts it, opens its
own login dialog inside the session and waits there, so ldm never
regains control and cannot say anything. With it, ldm asks the KDC first
and reports the actual reason in the greeter.

For a VM whose DRM device is not on the PCI bus (Hyper-V is one),
/etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/10-modesetting.conf in the image:

    Section "ServerFlags"
        Option "AutoAddGPU" "off"
    EndSection
    Section "Device"
        Identifier "Card0"
        Driver "modesetting"
        Option "kmsdev" "/dev/dri/card0"
    EndSection

Then build and publish:

    chroot "$B" update-initramfs -u -k all
    cp "$B"/boot/vmlinuz-* /srv/tftp/vmlinuz
    cp "$B"/boot/initrd.img-* /srv/tftp/initrd.img
    umount "$B/dev" "$B/proc" "$B/sys"
    mksquashfs "$B" /srv/ltsp/images/amd64.img -noappend -comp xz
    systemctl restart nbd-server

Expect roughly 660 MB. Note that the image keeps a root password and can
keep sshd for diagnostics; both are test scaffolding and must come out
before a production image.


Step 6 - The file server for home directories
-------------------------------------------------

    setup-home-server ltsplab.local LTSPLAB.LOCAL administrator \
        '<admin-password>' 172.17.240.0/20

Joins the domain, exports /srv/home over NFS with no_root_squash so
pam_mkhomedir can create directories, and - the part that matters - uses
the same ldap_id_mapping = True as the application servers, so every
host derives the same UID from the AD SID. Without that, a home
directory served to two servers has two different owners.


Step 7 - The application servers
------------------------------------

Run this on each application server, detached: it changes the address,
so a session running over the old one will be cut.

    setsid nohup setup-ltsp-app 172.17.250.31/20 ens18 172.17.240.1 \
        172.17.250.10 LTSPLAB.LOCAL administrator '<admin-password>' \
        172.17.250.40 > /root/setup.log 2>&1 &

Put the password in a small script file rather than on the command line
if you care about process listings and shell history.

What it does, and why each piece is there rather than left to defaults:

  static address and immutable resolv.conf
      Debian 13 ships dhcpcd, started by ifupdown rather than a systemd
      unit, so "systemctl is-enabled dhcpcd" says not-found while six
      dhcpcd processes are running. It rebinds a lease next to the static
      address, and it overwrites /etc/resolv.conf with an empty stub. The
      symptom is not a DNS error: sssd quietly drops offline, and from
      then on new domain accounts report "no such user" while already
      cached ones keep working.

  a [realms] block in krb5.conf
      default_realm alone leaves kinit reporting "Cannot find KDC for
      realm", because the shipped krb5.conf only knows the MIT examples.

  chrony against the DC
      Kerberos rejects tickets more than five minutes out. A member that
      drifts simply stops being able to log anyone in.

  ethtool, then the offload unit
      In nested virtualisation, checksum offload corrupts UDP and breaks
      DHCP. The unit that turns it off was present on both servers for
      days while ethtool was not installed, so it was enabled, visible
      and permanently "failed". An enabled unit is not evidence.

  xe on the glamor allow list
      Only relevant later, and inert today - see the GPU note below.

  xscreensaver instead of light-locker
      light-locker locks by way of the logind seat, and an xrdp session
      has no seat of its own. The xfce4 power manager goes too: nothing
      to manage on a session server, and one polling copy per session
      adds up on a host carrying many.

  the orphaned-session reaper
      See the traps section - this one made every login fail invisibly.

  adcli join, not realmd
      realmd verifies its dependencies through PackageKit, which a
      minimal Debian host does not have, so it refuses the join with
      "Necessary packages are not installed" while listing packages that
      are all installed. adcli creates the machine account and keytab,
      which is all that is needed.

  sssd with the GPO settings
      Without ad_gpo_map_remote_interactive = +xrdp-sesman, a correct
      password still fails with "pam_acct_mgmt failed: Permission
      denied", because sssd's GPO access control runs enforcing and maps
      every PAM service it does not recognise to Denied - regardless of
      what AD actually says.

Verify on each:

    klist -k /etc/krb5.keytab | grep -i "host/$(hostname -s)@"
    id administrator            # same UID on every host, or /home breaks
    findmnt -no SOURCE,FSTYPE /home
    systemctl is-active xrdp xrdp-sesman sssd
    systemctl is-active ltsp-nic-offload     # must be active, not failed


Step 8 - The load balancers
-------------------------------

Addresses first, because keepalived names its peer by address:

    setup-lb-addressing 172.17.250.51/20 172.17.250.52 ens18 \
        172.17.240.1 172.17.250.10        # on lb1
    setup-lb-addressing 172.17.250.52/20 172.17.250.51 ens18 \
        172.17.240.1 172.17.250.10        # on lb2

Then deploy server/doc/examples/haproxy-rdp.cfg, identical on both, with
the real addresses filled in. The two things to get right:

    backend rdp_prod
        balance leastconn
        stick-table type ip size 200k expire 12h
        stick on src
        option tcp-check
        server ltsp-app1 172.17.250.31:3389 check inter 5s rise 2 fall 3
        server ltsp-app2 172.17.250.32:3389 check inter 5s rise 2 fall 3

leastconn is the load balancing; the stick-table is the affinity. Do not
use "balance source" instead: an IP hash never looks at how many sessions
a server already carries, and because it is recomputed from the current
server list it pulls a failed-over client back the moment its original
server returns, tearing down a healthy session. A stick-table entry is
replaced only when it expires or its server goes away.

If several users can share one source address (NAT, a multi-user host),
key the table on the RDP cookie instead - "stick on
req.rdp_cookie(mstshash)" with a string table - which carries the
username. Not needed when each thin client has its own address, and the
source-IP form needs no protocol inspection delay.

An admin socket is worth having from the start:

    global
        stats socket /run/haproxy/admin.sock mode 660 level admin
        stats timeout 30s

Always validate before reloading. A broad regex over this file cost both
configs once:

    haproxy -c -f /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg && systemctl reload haproxy

keepalived, both VIPs in one instance because the same HAProxy serves
both:

    vrrp_instance VI_RDP_LB {
        state MASTER                  # BACKUP on lb2
        priority 150                  # 100 on lb2
        interface ens18
        virtual_router_id 51
        unicast_src_ip 172.17.250.51  # swap on lb2
        unicast_peer { 172.17.250.52 }
        virtual_ipaddress {
            172.17.249.222/20
            172.17.249.223/20
        }
        track_script { chk_haproxy }
    }

Unicast, because a virtual switch does not relay VRRP multicast between
guests. That works - but it names each peer by address, which is exactly
why step one of this section is static addressing. When those addresses
went stale, neither node saw the other, both promoted themselves, both
answered ARP for the VIP, and RDP sessions died five to nine seconds in.

Check that exactly one node holds them:

    ip -4 -o addr show | grep -E '172\.17\.249\.22[23]'


Step 9 - Domain users
-------------------------

    samba-tool user create test1 '<password>'

The default domain policy wants at least seven characters and
complexity; "test1" as a password is rejected. Either use compliant
passwords or relax the policy deliberately - do not discover this
halfway through a login test.


Acceptance tests
--------------------

These are the tests that actually caught something. Run them in this
order; each one assumes the previous passed.

1. The session survives, repeatedly.

    for target in 172.17.250.31 172.17.250.32 172.17.249.222; do
      for n in 1 2 3; do
        s=$(date +%s)
        timeout 12 xfreerdp3 /v:$target /u:test1 /p:'<pw>' /cert:ignore
        echo "$target try $n: $(( $(date +%s) - s ))s"
      done
    done

   A session that ends after one or two seconds with
   ERRINFO_LOGOFF_BY_USER is the orphaned-session trap, not a user
   logging off. Twelve seconds means it held until the test cut it.

2. Distribution - and this is the one that needs care. A test from a
   single machine sends every connection from one source address, so
   everything lands on one backend and the other stays at zero. That
   looks like a passed test. Use several source addresses:

    for ip in 172.17.250.60 172.17.250.61 172.17.250.62 \
              172.17.250.63 172.17.250.64 172.17.250.65; do
        ip addr add "$ip/20" dev vmbr0
        ( nc -s "$ip" -w 60 172.17.249.222 3389 >/dev/null 2>&1 ) &
    done
    echo 'show stat'  | socat /run/haproxy/admin.sock stdio   # on the LB
    echo 'show table rdp_prod' | socat /run/haproxy/admin.sock stdio

   Expect the connections split evenly, and every source address named
   in the table against a server. Disconnect, reconnect, and check the
   assignments are unchanged while the totals rise. Count per server -
   counting successes proves nothing here.

3. Failover.

    systemctl stop xrdp        # on one application server
    # within ~20s (inter 5s, fall 3) it must read DOWN, the others UP,
    # and a login through the VIP must still succeed
    systemctl start xrdp       # back to UP

4. Staging isolation, in both directions.

    # six connections to VIP_PROD: only the production pool's counters move
    # six connections to VIP_STAGING: only the staging pool's counters move
    # stop xrdp in the staging pool: production stays UP and reachable
    # stop xrdp on ALL production backends: staging stays UP and reachable

   Both halves matter. A bad staging rollout must not touch production,
   and a production outage must not take staging down with it.

5. Shared identity and home directories.

    id test1        # identical UID on every application server
    touch /home/test1/.probe        # on one server
    ls /home/test1/.probe           # visible on the others and on home1

6. RAM operation on a booted client.

    findmnt | grep -E 'toram|rofs'
    dmesg | grep -i nbd_disconnect


Traps, and how each one announces itself
--------------------------------------------

Sorted by how much time each cost, worst first.

  A successful login that ends after two seconds, with no error
  anywhere. The client is told ERRINFO_LOGOFF_BY_USER - "the user logged
  off". What actually happened: an xfce4-session from an earlier login
  is still running and holds the org.xfce.SessionManager name on the
  user's bus, which lives in /run/user/<uid> and survives between
  sessions. The new xfce4-session finds the name taken and exits 0; xrdp
  sees its window manager finish cleanly and ends the session. Every
  link in that chain behaves correctly, which is why nothing is logged.
  Look for "exited quickly" in xrdp-sesman.log, and for a stray
  xfce4-session:

    pgrep -a -u "$(id -u test1)" xfce4-session

  Orphans are created by restarting xrdp-sesman while sessions run.
  setup-ltsp-app installs a reaper as an ExecStartPre of xrdp-sesman,
  where the rule is sound: before sesman starts it tracks nothing, so
  any process still carrying XRDP_SESSION belongs to a lost session. The
  same rule on a running server would be wrong.

  A guest that looks dead but is only waiting. /home is a hard NFS
  mount. Lose the address and every login blocks in the kernel waiting
  for a file server it can no longer route to - sshd included - so the
  machine answers nothing and the console shows nothing. The previous
  boot's journal tells the story: the address being withdrawn, then
  hundreds of "nfs: server not responding". Keep the mount hard anyway;
  a soft mount turns a server outage into short writes and silently
  damaged files in people's home directories. What has to change is that
  no configuration step may remove an address and hope ifupdown restores
  it.

  A wrong password with no way back. xrdp has no server-side NLA, so it
  accepts the connection and validates inside the session, in its own
  dialog. Measured against xrdp 0.10.1, the connection stayed open a
  full minute after AUTHFAIL. Reading the RDP client's exit status does
  not help - nothing has failed yet. RDP_PREAUTH asks the KDC before
  starting the client instead.

  Load balancing that is only affinity. "balance source" distributes by
  nothing. See step 8, and test 2 for why it is hard to notice.

  Both load balancers holding the same VIP. Stale unicast peer
  addresses. Sessions die five to nine seconds in, HAProxy logs the
  abort in the data phase (CD), and the session server shows the session
  still running happily.

  DHCP that never succeeds, on nested virtualisation. Checksum offload.
  "ethtool -K <if> tx off rx off", and make it persistent - as a pre-up
  in /etc/network/interfaces if you want it to cover the very first
  address, not just later traffic.

  Xorg respawning forever on the thin client. LTSP's
  50-rm-system-services used to delete systemd-logind from the image.
  Xorg 21 needs a logind session to get its DRM and input file
  descriptors. Also check the colour depth: 16bpp is right for old
  hardware and rejected by modern KMS drivers ("failed to add fb -22"),
  so it must only be the default when there is no /dev/dri/card0.

  A frozen console that is not frozen. Two identical screenshots of a
  VM console can be one stale framebuffer. Switch VTs, or ping the
  client, before concluding anything.

  A machine that "cannot join the realm" while every package is
  installed. realmd and PackageKit. Use adcli.

  A join that reports success but produced nothing. Do not grep the
  keytab for the hostname: adcli also writes RestrictedKrbHost entries,
  so a plain hostname match reports "already joined" for a host that
  never joined. Match "host/<name>@".


Recovery: getting into a guest that has no address
------------------------------------------------------

This happens, and typing into a VM console is not always an option. Edit
the disk offline instead:

    qm stop 213
    modprobe nbd max_part=8
    qemu-nbd --connect=/dev/nbd0 /var/lib/vz/images/213/vm-213-disk-0.qcow2
    mount /dev/nbd0p1 /mnt/app3
    # write /etc/network/interfaces, /etc/resolv.conf, whatever is needed
    umount /mnt/app3
    qemu-nbd --disconnect /dev/nbd0
    qm start 213

Always "sync" before stopping a guest you have just written files to.
Killing a guest right after writing configuration loses it.


A note on GPUs, for later
-----------------------------

If sessions should use a GPU, two things are in the way and only one is
solved. xorgxrdp gates glamor behind an allow list of DRM driver names -
Option "DRMAllowList" in /etc/X11/xrdp/xorg.conf - which Debian ships as
"i915 radeon". Intel's discrete Arc B-series (Battlemage) is driven only
by xe, never i915: against Debian 13's kernel 6.12.94, the Battlemage PCI
ids E20B/E20C/E20D/E202/E212 appear in xe's alias list and in none of
i915's. setup-ltsp-app adds xe to that list, and upstream's devel branch
now carries "amdgpu i915 xe msm radeon" as well.

The unsolved part: Debian builds xorgxrdp without glamor at all -
libxorgxrdp.so contains no glamor, EGL, GBM or DRI3 symbols - so GPU use
needs a self-built xorgxrdp with --enable-glamor, which upstream still
calls experimental. That is a decision about carrying your own package
for every session server.

Keep two things apart while planning: glamor accelerates rendering
inside the session, while the RDP stream's encoding stays in software
(rfxcodec/openh264; there is no GPU encoding upstream). And the Arc
B-series has no SR-IOV, so under Proxmox a card passes through to
exactly one VM - GPU-accelerated sessions concentrate on whichever
application server holds it, which is a question for the load balancing
and not only for the driver.


File inventory
